\label{ex:A7}
In this example we show that, to guarantee the nonsingularity of each $\widehat \mathbf{C}_n$, it is not enough that the family $\{\mathbf{C}_n\}_n$ is weakly well-conditioned and that the top-left and bottom-right blocks of size $(m+k) \times (m+k)$ of each element of $\{\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_n\}_n$ are full rank. Consider the family $\{\mathbf{C}_n\}_n$ with $c_{-3} = 1, c_{-2} = 2, c_{-1} = -6, c_0 = 2$ and $c_1 = 1$. The associated polynomial $q^{\mathbf{C}}(z) = 1 + 2z - 6z^2 + 2z^3 + z^4$ is of type $(1,2,1)$, since it has roots $-2 \pm \sqrt{3}$ and double root $1$. Therefore, the family $\{\mathbf{C}_n\}_n$ is weakly well-conditioned. Consider the family of perturbed matrices $\{\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_n\}_n$ with perturbation only in the top-left block defined as

\begin{equation*}
\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_n = 
\setcounter{MaxMatrixCols}{20}
    \begin{pmatrix} 
            -6 & 2  \\
            -8 & 1 & 1 \\
            1 & -6 & 2 & 1 \\
            1 & 2 & -6 & 2 & 1 \\
            & \ddots & \ddots & \ddots & \ddots & \ddots \\
             & & 1 & 2 & -6 & 2 & 1 \\
             & & & 1 & 2 & -6 & 2 \\
    \end{pmatrix}_{n \times n} \hspace{-0.5cm}.
\end{equation*}

According to the notation in the proof of Theorem \ref{th:45}, we can compute with \eqref{eq:A15}

\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{G}_2^{(1)} = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 & -\frac{3}{2} & - \frac{1}{2} \vspace{0.1cm}\\
-\frac{2}{3} & -1 & -\frac{1}{3} \vspace{0.1cm} \\
-\frac{23}{18} \sqrt{3} - \frac{13}{6} & -\frac{19}{12} \sqrt{3} - \frac{11}{4} & \frac{19}{36} \sqrt{3} + \frac{11}{12} \\
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

associated with the family $\{\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_n\}_n$. This matrix is not full rank. Therefore, for $n$ sufficiently large, each $\widehat{\mathbf{C}}_n$ is singular, even if the top-left block of size $4 \times 4$ of each $\widehat \mathbf{C}_n$ is full rank.
